{{distinguish|Technocriticism}}

'''Criticism of technology''' is an analysis of adverse impacts of industrial and [[digital electronics|digital]] technologies. It is argued that, in all advanced industrial societies (not necessarily only capitalist ones), technology becomes a means of domination, control, and exploitation,<ref>{{Cite book|title=History and Philosophy of Science and Technology|last1=Lorenzano|first1=Pablo|authorlink2=Hans-Jörg Rheinberger|last2=Rheinberger|first2=Hans-Jörg|last3=Ortiz|first3=Eduardo|last4=Galles|first4=Carlos|publisher=EOLSS Publishers Co. Ltd.|year=2010|isbn=9781848267763|location=Oxford|pages=124–125}}</ref> or more generally something which threatens the survival of humanity.  Some of the technology opposed by the most radical critics may include everyday household products, such as [[refrigerator]]s, [[Home computer|computers]], and [[medication]].<ref name="GlendinningChellis">[[Chellis Glendinning|Glendinning, Chellis]]. ''[https://theanarchistlibrary.org/library/chellis-glendinning-notes-toward-a-neo-luddite-manifesto Notes towards a Neo-Luddite manifesto]''. Utne Reader, 1990.</ref> However, criticism of technology comes in many shades.

{{More sources needed|date=September 2024}}{{short description|Compound that participates in a chemical reaction that produces another compound}}
In [[chemistry]], a '''precursor''' is a [[chemical compound|compound]] that participates in a [[chemical reaction]] that produces another compound.

In [[biochemistry]], the term "precursor" often refers more specifically to a chemical compound preceding another in a [[metabolic pathway]], such as a [[protein precursor]].

{{Short description|Literature mainly written for or by the working class}}
'''Proletarian literature''' refers here to the literature created by [[left-wing]] writers mainly for the [[class-consciousness|class-conscious]] [[proletariat]]. Though the ''[[Encyclopædia Britannica]]'' states that because it "is essentially an intended device of revolution", it is therefore often published, though not necessarily written, by [[Communist Party]] or left-wing sympathizers,<ref>{{Cite news |title=Novel {{!}} Definition, Elements, Examples, Types, & Facts {{!}} Britannica |url=https://www.britannica.com/art/novel |access-date=2026-06-25 |work=Encyclopedia Britannica |language=en}}</ref> the proletarian novel has also been categorized without any emphasis on revolution, as a novel "about the working classes and working-class life; perhaps with the intention of making propaganda".<ref>J. A. Cuddon, ''A Dictionary of Literary Terms and Literary Criticism''. (London: Penguin Books, 1999, p. 703.</ref> This different emphasis may reflect a difference between Russian, American and other traditions of working-class writing, with that of Britain. The British tradition was not especially inspired by the Communist Party, but had its roots in the [[Chartist movement]], and [[socialism]], amongst others.<ref>Ian Hayward, ''Working-Class Fiction: from Chartism to 'Trainspotting{{'-}}''. (London: Northcote House, 1997), pp. 1-3</ref> Furthermore, writing about the British working-class writers, H Gustav Klaus, in ''The Socialist Novel: Towards the Recovery of a Tradition'' (1982) suggested that "the once current [term] 'proletarian' is, internationally, on the retreat, while the competing concepts of 'working-class' and 'socialist' continue to command about equal adherence".<ref>Brighton: Harvest Press, 1982, p. 1.</ref>

The word proletarian is also used, more narrowly, to describe works about the working class by working-class authors, to distinguish them from works by middle-class authors such as [[Charles Dickens]] (''[[Hard Times (novel)|Hard Times]]''), [[John Steinbeck]] (''[[The Grapes of Wrath]]''), and [[Henry Green]] (''[[Living (novel)|Living]]'').<ref name="Fordham-p71">John Fordham, "'A Strange Field’: Region and Class in the Novels of Harold Heslop" in ''Intermodernism: Literary Culture in Mid-Twentieth-Century Britain'', ed. Kristin Bluemel. Published 2009 :Edinburgh University Press, note no.1, p. 71.</ref> [[William Blake]] (1757–1827) was also of the middle-class, but some of his works are early examples of working-class literature, including the two "The Chimney Sweeper" poems, published in ''[[Songs of Innocence]]'' in 1789 and ''[[Songs of Experience]]'' in 1794, which deal with the subject of [[child labour]].<ref>"[http://asms.k12.ar.us/classes/humanities/britlit/97-98/blake/POEMS.htm William Blake Page] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070521071628/http://asms.k12.ar.us/classes/humanities/britlit/97-98/blake/POEMS.htm |date=2007-05-21 }}", The Arkansas School for Mathematics, Sciences, & the Arts</ref>

{{short description|Property of an atomic or molecular energy level}}
In [[spectroscopy]] and [[quantum chemistry]], the '''multiplicity''' of an [[energy level]] is defined as ''2S+1'', where ''S'' is the [[Spin quantum number#Total spin of an atom or molecule|
total spin angular momentum]].{{sfn|Atkins|de Paula|2006|p=353}}{{sfn|Levine|1991|p=308}}<ref name=IUPAC>{{cite book |chapter-url=http://goldbook.iupac.org/html/M/M04062.html |chapter=multiplicity (spin multiplicity) |date=2006 |publisher=IUPAC ([[International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry]]) |access-date=30 March 2018 |doi=10.1351/goldbook.M04062  |title=IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology |isbn=0-9678550-9-8 }}</ref> States with multiplicity 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 are respectively called [[singlet state|singlets]], [[Doublet state|doublets]], [[triplet state|triplets]], quartets and quintets.{{sfn|Levine|1991|p=308}}

In the [[ground state]] of an atom or molecule, the unpaired electrons usually all have parallel spin. In this case the multiplicity is also equal to the number of unpaired electrons plus one.{{sfn|Miessler|Tarr|1999|p=33}}

{{Short description|Slow section of a Hungarian folk dance}}
{{for|the town in Germany|Lassan, Germany}}
{{Wiktionary|lassú}}

'''Lassan''' ([[Hungarian language|Hungarian]] for "slowly") or more properly '''lassú''' ("slow") is the slow section of the ''[[csárdás]]'', a Hungarian [[folk dance]], or of most of [[Franz Liszt|Liszt's]] ''[[Hungarian Rhapsodies]]'', which take their form from this dance.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Horn |first=David |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=WKc0DwAAQBAJ |title=Bloomsbury Encyclopedia of Popular Music of the World, Volume 11: Genres: Europe |date=2017-10-05 |publisher=Bloomsbury Publishing USA |isbn=978-1-5013-2610-3 |pages=182 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Piotrowska |first=Anna G. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=8nMiAgAAQBAJ |title=Gypsy Music in European Culture: From the Late Eighteenth to the Early Twentieth Centuries |date=2013-12-03 |publisher=Northeastern University Press |isbn=978-1-55553-837-8 |pages=207 |language=en}}</ref> It generally either has a dark, somber tone or a formal, stately one.

{{Short description|Literary movement}}
{{Original research|date=September 2009}}

As a [[Mode (literature)|literary mode]], '''sentimentalism''', the practice of being [[Sentimentality|sentimental]], and thus tending towards making emotions and feelings the basis of a person's actions and reactions, as opposed to reason,<ref name="OED">"sentimentalism, n.", ''[[Oxford English Dictionary]]''</ref> has been a recurring aspect of world literature. Sentimentalism includes a variety of aspects in literature, such as sentimental poetry, the sentimental novel, and the German sentimentalist music movement, [[Sensitive style|Empfindsamkeit]]. European literary sentimentalism arose during the [[Age of Enlightenment]], partly as a response to [[Moral sense theory|sentimentalism in philosophy]]. In eighteenth-century [[England]], the [[sentimental novel]] was a major literary genre. The genre developed in England between 1730 and 1780 at the time of high enlightenment from where it spread to other European literatures.<ref name="Jugoslovenski književni leksikon">{{cite book |author= Dragiša Živković |editor = Živan Milisavac |date=1971 |title=Jugoslovenski književni leksikon |trans-title=Yugoslav Literary Lexicon |publisher=[[Matica srpska]] |language=sh |location= [[Novi Sad]] ([[Socialist Autonomous Province of Vojvodina|SAP Vojvodina]], [[Socialist Republic of Serbia|SR Serbia]]) |page=476-478 }}</ref> Its philosophical basis primarily came from [[Anthony Ashley Cooper, 3rd Earl of Shaftesbury]], a pupil of [[John Locke]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.britannica.com/biography/John-Locke|title=John Locke - Association with Shaftesbury|website=Encyclopedia Britannica|language=en|access-date=2019-12-12}}</ref>

