{{Short description|Development of female characteristics}}
{{Sex (biology) sidebar}}
In [[biology]] and [[medicine]], '''feminization''' is the development in an [[organism]] of physical characteristics that are usually unique to the [[female]]s of the [[species]]. This may represent a normal [[development (biology)|developmental]] process, contributing to [[sexual differentiation]]. Feminization can also be induced by environmental factors, and this phenomenon has been observed in several animal species.<ref name="Fry-1981">{{cite journal |last1=Fry |first1=D. |last2=Toone |first2=C. |title=DDT-induced feminization of gull embryos |journal=Science |date=21 August 1981 |volume=213 |issue=4510 |pages=922–924 |doi=10.1126/science.7256288 |pmid=7256288 |bibcode=1981Sci...213..922F }}</ref><ref name="Gimeno-1996">{{cite journal |last1=Gimeno |first1=Sylvia |last2=Gerritsen |first2=Anton |last3=Bowmer |first3=Tim |last4=Komen |first4=Hans |title=Feminization of male carp |journal=Nature |date=November 1996 |volume=384 |issue=6606 |pages=221–222 |doi=10.1038/384221a0 |pmid=8918871 |bibcode=1996Natur.384..221G |s2cid=7497542 }}</ref> In the case of [[feminizing hormone therapy]], it is intentionally induced medically.

{{Short description|Medieval literary genre}}
[[File:BL Royal Vincent of Beauvais.jpg|thumb|right|This [[author portrait]] of [[Vincent of Beauvais]] in a manuscript of his ''Speculum Historiale'', contains an actual convex mirror as a visual pun. French translation by [[Jean de Vignay]], Bruges, c. 1478–1480, for [[Edward IV of England|Edward IV]]; [[British Library]]]]

The medieval genre of '''speculum literature''', popular from the twelfth through the sixteenth centuries, was inspired by the urge to encompass [[encyclopedia|encyclopedic]] knowledge within a single work.<ref>{{citation |last=Bradley |first=Ritamary|title=Backgrounds of the Title Speculum in Mediaeval Literature |journal=Speculum |volume=29 |issue=1 |date=January 1954 |pages=100–115 |doi=10.2307/2853870 |jstor=2853870|s2cid=163144785 }}.</ref> However, some of these works have a restricted scope and function as instructional manuals.<ref>{{cite book | last = Franklin-Brown | first = Mary | title = Reading the world: encyclopedic writing in the scholastic age | publisher = The University of Chicago Press | location = Chicago London | year = 2012 | isbn = 9780226260709|page=273}}</ref> In this sense, the encyclopedia and the speculum are similar but they are not the same genre.{{sfn|Franklin-Brown|2012|p=273}}

{{Short description|Branch of organometallic chemistry}}
[[File:Organonickel.png|right|150px|organonickel]]
'''Organonickel chemistry''' is a branch of [[organometallic chemistry]] that deals with [[organic compound]]s featuring [[nickel]]-[[carbon]] bonds.<ref>F.A. Carey R.J. Sundberg ''Advanced Organic Chemistry'' 2nd Ed. {{ISBN|0-306-41199-7}}</ref><ref>''Comprehensive organometallic chemistry'' III Robert Crabtree, Mike Mingos 2006  {{ISBN|0-08-044590-X}}</ref> They are used as a catalyst, as a building block in organic chemistry and in [[chemical vapor deposition]]. Organonickel compounds are also short-lived intermediates in organic reactions. The first organonickel compound was [[nickel tetracarbonyl]] Ni(CO)<sub>4</sub>, reported in 1890 and quickly applied in the [[Mond process]] for nickel purification.  Organonickel complexes are prominent in numerous industrial processes including [[carbonylation]]s, [[hydrocyanation]], and the [[Shell higher olefin process]].<ref>{{cite journal |doi=10.1021/acscatal.5b00072|title=Nickel: The "Spirited Horse" of Transition Metal Catalysis|year=2015|last1=Ananikov|first1=Valentine P.|journal=ACS Catalysis|volume=5|issue=3|pages=1964–1971|doi-access=free}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Tasker |first1=Sarah Z. |last2=Standley |first2=Eric A. |last3=Jamison |first3=Timothy F. |title=Recent Advances in Homogeneous Nickel Catalysis |journal=Nature |volume=509 |issue=7500 |pages=299–309 |doi=10.1038/nature13274 |pmc=4344729 |pmid=24828188|year=2014 |bibcode=2014Natur.509..299T}}</ref>

